    "response": "In short, **no—making an issue of the all-male panel will not help your case and is unlikely to be evidence of unfair bias against you.** \n\nHere is a breakdown of why, what the composition of the panel actually signifies, and what other factors are likely at play.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Does the All-Male Panel Signify Anything for Your Candidacy?\n\n* **Not Gender Discrimination Against You:** Because both you and the successful candidate are men, the gender composition of the panel does not indicate gender-based bias against you. \n* **Reflective of Industry Demographics:** Structural engineering leadership—especially at the partner, director, and senior management levels—remains heavily male-dominated. An all-male interview panel is very common in traditional consultancies, even if many firms are trying to diversify their hiring boards.\n* **Potential for \"Fit\" and Homogeneity:** What an all-male or homogeneous panel *can* sometimes signal is a traditional, insular corporate culture. In such environments, subjective criteria like \"culture fit,\" personal rapport, or being part of an existing network can heavily sway decisions. However, this is rarely actionable or provable.\n\n---\n\n### 2. What the Selection Clues Really Point To\n\nGiven the details you shared, the decision almost certainly came down to factors other than gender:\n\n* **Commercial Focus Over Technical Seniority:**\n  * For a **Head of Group** role, the remit often shifts from pure technical excellence to business development, winning bids, and managing key accounts.\n  * The firm’s public announcement specifically highlighting that he has **\"excellent client relationships\"** is a major clue. Consultancies often prioritize someone who can immediately generate or protect revenue streams over someone with more design and technical leadership experience.\n* **Years of Experience vs. Adaptability/Cost:**\n  * You have 15 years of experience and three major project leads; he has 8 years. \n  * While 8 years is on the leaner side for a department head, the firm may have seen him as someone who is energetic, mouldable, or willing to accept a compensation package lower than what a 15-year veteran with a master’s degree would command.\n* **Internal Dynamics or Existing Ties:**\n  * In many cases where an ostensibly less-experienced candidate wins a senior role on the basis of \"client relationships,\" they either:\n    1. Had pre-existing connections with the panel or major clients.\n    2. Were an internal candidate or brought an existing book of business/clients with them.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Why You Haven't Heard Back in Two Weeks\n\n* **HR Risk Management:** Companies are notoriously cautious about providing **written** explanations to unsuccessful finalists, especially when the successful candidate has fewer years of experience. Providing written criteria opens the door to potential disputes (such as age discrimination inquiries).\n* **Corporate Inertia:** Two weeks is a standard delay in corporate HR timelines. Once a role is accepted, wrapping up administrative feedback for rejected applicants often drops to the bottom of the priority list.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Pivot from \"Written Explanation\" to a \"Brief Phone Call\":**\n   * People are reluctant to put candid hiring rationales in writing for legal reasons, but they are often willing to speak informally.\n   * Send a polite, constructive follow-up:\n     > *\"I understand you are busy, but I would greatly appreciate a brief 10-minute call for any constructive feedback on how I can strengthen my profile for similar leadership opportunities in the future.\"*\n2. **Focus Your Questions on Role Requirements:**\n   * If you secure a call, ask questions like:\n     * *\"Was the balance of the role weighted more toward client acquisition and business development than technical delivery?\"*\n     * *\"What specific capabilities or perspectives set the successful candidate apart?\"*\n3. **Protect Your Professional Reputation:**\n   * The structural engineering community at senior levels is relatively small. Raising issues about panel composition without clear legal grounds risks coming across as bitter, which could hurt your reputation with peer firms and clients. \n   * Acknowledge the outcome graciously, keep the door open, and focus your search on firms looking for deep technical authority combined with project leadership.\n",
